      "nets": ["+5V", "RST"],
      "observed": "R3 is a 10Ω resistor connected between +5V and the /RESET net, acting as the reset pull-up.",
      "expected": "A reset pull-up should be a high resistance, commonly around 10kΩ as used by R1 and R6, so that an ISP programmer or other driver can pull reset low without excessive current.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "R3 | 10 | Resistor_SMD:R_0805_2012Metric | Device:R_Small | Resistor, small symbol"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "RST : J1.4(Pin_4,passive) J4.5(~{RST},passive) R3.2(passive) U2.1(~{RESET}/PB5,bidirectional) U3.1(~{RESET}/PB5,bidirectional)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spice",
          "quote": "R3 +5V RST 10"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "R1 | 10K | Resistor_SMD:R_0805_2012Metric | Device:R_Small | Resistor, small symbol"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If reset is driven low, the 10Ω pull-up will pass about 0.5A from +5V to ground, likely preventing programming, overheating R3, and possibly damaging the regulator or programmer.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design contains a likely reset-pull-up value error: R3 is 10Ω from +5V to RST, whereas a high-value pull-up is required; this can overload the reset line when driven low."
}

